  {
    "episode_id": 80224,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/0/80224.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 31,
    "turn_text": "Well, Barry, I think that the personnel who are serving in Iraq need to have the funding necessary to do the job that they were sent to do. As you know, I voted against the unilateral invasion of Iraq. And as you know, I voted against the emergency supplemental appropriation of $87 billion. And I did it for a couple of reasons. It seems to me that there's got to be some accountability on this expenditure. And that's something that's absent right now. Given all the money that's been spent today, just another $80 billion had already been spent before this $87 billion appropriation was even introduced in the House or the Senate. And we don't know where that money was spent. I think that there's a lot of work that needs to be done to provide accountability on the private sector contracts that have been let, the non-bid private sector contracts. I think we need to know why it is our mechanized equipment doesn't have the parts that's necessary. We need to know why we have U.S. military personnel serving in Iraq without having Kevlar inserts in their body armor. We need to know why it is that families from across our district and Every other district in this country is having to send supplies to their sons and daughters and community members who are serving in Iraq. When you have to have a bake sale to raise money to buy socks for your son and his platoon, there's a problem and there's a definite lack of accountability and that needs to be discussed. We've got situations where we have American contractors that are bidding to do work and we end up subbing that work out to Iraqi individuals for a fraction of the cost. I think these are all questions that need to be asked and that is in large part why I voted against it.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_00",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "Personnel serving in Iraq require adequate funding to fulfill their mission.",
        "confidence": 0.96
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker voted against the $87 billion emergency supplemental appropriation.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker voted against the unilateral invasion of Iraq.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "Mechanized equipment used by U.S. forces lacks essential parts.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "There is currently no accountability regarding the expenditure of funds in Iraq.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "Families are sending supplies to service members because basic necessities are not provided.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "Accountability is needed for private sector contracts in Iraq, particularly non-bid contracts.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "Over $80 billion has already been spent on Iraq prior to the introduction of the $87 billion appropriation.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
