  {
    "episode_id": 80224,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/0/80224.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 59,
    "turn_text": "Well, Barry, you know what's wrong with the picture. That's a very rhetorical question. There was a movement by this administration. that led us into this war and it's been called by others a war of choice. I, as you know, from the beginning I expressed concern, have never believed that we were in any immediate danger from Iraq, voted against the war resolution to go to Iraq for that purpose, sat through all the top secret briefings where this administration tried to make a case as to why it was necessary for us to go there, and was never convinced that there was a reason to do a preemptive unilateral strike of a sovereign country. And voted accordingly. The administration, as you know, stated that we were in immediate danger, that Saddam Hussein had massive weapons of mass destruction, that he was very, very close to building if he hadn't already built a nuclear weapon and made their case that we needed to go in there and do it unilaterally. And that was the vote of Congress. And that's what brought us there. And as you know, subsequent to that we have not found any indication that there were anything in that there was anything in that country that put us at any immediate danger. You know that we the US military and the British military moved so quickly that we crossed the proverbial red line into Baghdad faster than anyone had anticipated. So if there were any weapons of mass destruction, there's no way they could have been hidden. We were there too quickly for that to happen. And it's clear that that was not the case. And either someone misled us terribly or the intelligence under which we were using to operate was so badly flawed that they missed this thing by 180 degrees.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_00",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
